  "limitations": [
    "Single-view TRELLIS reconstruction from one approved concept: the far side, back and underside are TRELLIS's invention, with no rear-view painting projected onto them.",
    "Roughness, metalness and ambient occlusion are derived from colour inside declared regions and Cycles bakes, not measured PBR or hand-painted masks.",
    "One body mesh with its detail painted into a single base colour (cooked at 1024 px, shipped at 512 px); there are no LODs, trim sheets or separate material layers.",
    "The base colour is lifted by luma power 0.8 (chroma kept), set by eye in review renders, not calibrated against the concept.",
    "Re-cooking is not guaranteed to be byte-reproducible: MikkTSpace tangents can round differently on a few vertices, and Blender's smart UV project can lay out an atlas differently between runs.",
    "Hooves are single caps without cloven toes, the eyes are painted, the collar is part of the body mesh and bends with the neck, and the tail is fused along the rump, so its four tail joints can only sway it stiffly.",
    "Bone-heat skinning (32 joints, 4 influences) with procedural clips authored in Blender, no motion capture and no root motion. Measured on every 60 Hz frame: single edges stretch up to 5.5x (Walk), 6.35x (Trot) and 5.87x (Canter) at the shoulder-spine crease, and 1.99x at the 99.5th percentile in the Canter (limit 2.0); planted hooves slide up to 0.10 m/s.",
    "The ox trots at the shipment's 4 m/s and canters at the convoy's 4.7 to 6.95 m/s; a real draught ox walks at about 1 m/s. The game's speeds were not changed, so the gait is fast for the animal.",
    "The ox never takes a faction dye (an empty mask on the troops' skinned program), so both wagons use the same ox."
